  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>&#8652;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></mrow></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Given</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>28</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Number</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>moles</mi></math></p><p>To find the limiting reagent we can divide given number of moles with Stoichiometric coefficients, whichever will be the lower will be limiting reagent.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>28</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>28</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is the Limiting Reagent.</p><p>According to the reaction one mole of nitrogen gas gives two moles of ammonia.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mfenced><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>28</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Number of moles of ammonia formed will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>42</mn></math></p></blockquote>",
